{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '1\\\\.5|1\\\\.7|DX([1-9]|1[0-2])\\\\b|React adapter|commit contract' docs/0{2-product-spec,3-plan-typescript-release}.md\"","aggregated_output":"docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:5:This plan builds the product defined in [02-product-spec.md](02-product-spec.md). Criterion IDs (F1, L1, A2, DX4, P1 …) refer to that spec; finding IDs (R5, D2 …) refer to [01-landscape.md](01-landscape.md).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:34:| One function per chart type (`line`, `area`, `bar`, `donut`, `scatter`) | Each has its own options type, so options that do not apply are type errors (DX3), and bundlers can drop unused chart types. Whether shared code actually tree-shakes is measured on real consumer builds in M7. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:35:| Plain options object, not a builder or a JSX tree | The same object works in the React adapter. Recharts needed eight components for the trend task; the information content was four fields. |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:101:| `colors` | A fixed color per series name (DX6). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:107:### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:149:### 1.7 React adapter\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:299:- React adapter prototype, used to test the commit contract (1.5, 1.7).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:338:- `update`, `append` and `zoom` under the full commit contract; pause; simplified drawing complete; zoom by drag, buttons, keyboard and opt-in touch.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:341:- Sequence tests from DX5.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:353:- The packed tarball installed into three clean fixtures (npm with a bundler, script tag, React): the first chart works in ten lines or fewer in each (DX1).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:354:- Node import and `validate()` without a DOM; SSR hydration; strict CSP with real headers (DX8).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:355:- Size measured on the packed distribution for line-only, full, script-tag and React cases, CSS included (DX7).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:356:- Every runnable docs example compiled and rendered from the packed distribution (DX10).\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:357:- The agent rehearsal (DX12), recorded as rehearsal.\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:380:| Types | `docs/api/examples.ts` pattern | The listed mistakes fail to compile (DX3). |\ndocs/03-plan-typescript-release.md:381:| Packed distribution | Clean install fixtures | What users actually get (DX1, DX7, DX8, DX10). |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:11:It is a framework-neutral TypeScript library under the MIT license, with a thin React adapter.\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:158:| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:222:| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm, by script tag and in React. No required option besides data and fields. | Three CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:223:| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:224:| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:225:| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:226:| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:227:| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:228:| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, provisional until M1 measures a working slice: 40 KiB gzipped for everything, 25 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, 2 KiB for the React adapter, CSS included. | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:229:| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Works under React StrictMode, including setup, cleanup and setup again. Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:230:| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:231:| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:232:| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:233:| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:246:| P6 | Download | Minified and gzipped bytes, CSS included | Exact | DX7 | 01, section 1.4 |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:269:| Three methods with a commit contract | D3. | Changed from \"one update method\": `append` and `zoom` were always there, and their ordering is now specified. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:277:| React adapter in 1.0 | Recharts leads npm installs among chart-level libraries. | Kept, and built early to test the core API. Downloads show where charts are written, not that Axylo will be wanted there. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:285:| Test with screen-reader users, outside developers or design partners before release | One-person project with no participants. Recorded as a stated limit (3.8). Agent rehearsal (DX12) and developer rehearsal (A3) are used and labelled as such. |\ndocs/02-product-spec.md:288:| Remove the first-chart line-count criterion | Kept as a floor, and joined by DX12, which tests getting it right. |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
