{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | sed -n '40,188p'; nl -ba reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/react.report.md\"","aggregated_output":"    40\t```ts\n    41\t// long rows: one row per (x, series)\n    42\tline(el, { data: rows, x: 'day', y: 'sessions', series: 'channel' });\n    43\t\n    44\t// wide rows: one column per series\n    45\tline(el, { data: wide, x: 'day', y: ['direct', 'organic', { field: 'referral', label: 'Referral' }] });\n    46\t\n    47\t// typed columns for large data; the row type is inferred from the columns\n    48\tline(el, { data: { columns: { t: new Float64Array(n), v: new Float32Array(n) } }, x: { field: 't', type: 'time' }, y: 'v' });\n    49\t```\n    50\t\n    51\tLong and wide are mutually exclusive in the types: `series` cannot be combined with an array of `y`.\n    52\t\n    53\tRules the three forms share:\n    54\t\n    55\t| Rule | Detail |\n    56\t|---|---|\n    57\t| Ownership | Row arrays are read once and never mutated. Typed columns are borrowed, not copied; the caller must not change them in place, and replaces them through `update`. |\n    58\t| Column lengths | All columns must have the same length; otherwise AX100. |\n    59\t| Missing values | Rows: `null`, or an absent key. Typed columns: `NaN` means missing and does not raise AX103. |\n    60\t| Order | Line, area and scatter are sorted by x internally (F11). `append` expects rows at or after the last x. |\n    61\t| Provenance | Every normalized sample keeps the index of the row it came from, so diagnostics, the table and events point at the caller's own data. |\n    62\t\n    63\t### 1.3 Fields\n    64\t\n    65\tA field is a key of the row, or an object when it needs more. The key is checked against the row type by role: a value axis accepts number fields; text fields are accepted there only with `parse: 'number'`.\n    66\t\n    67\t```ts\n    68\tline(el, {\n    69\t  data: samples,\n    70\t  x: { field: 'ts', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' },\n    71\t  y: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'sessions', tickFormat: { notation: 'compact' } },\n    72\t});\n    73\t\n    74\t// counts that a database driver returns as strings\n    75\tbar(el, { data: pgRows, x: 'day', y: { field: 'count', parse: 'number', missing: 'zero' } });\n    76\t```\n    77\t\n    78\t| Option | Meaning |\n    79\t|---|---|\n    80\t| `field` | Key of the row. |\n    81\t| `label`, `unit` | Axis title; name and unit in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n    82\t| `type` (x) | `'date'`, `'time'`, `'number'`, `'category'`. Inferred when omitted, except that a number is never inferred to be an instant (F4). |\n    83\t| `timeZone` (x) | For instants: `'local'` (default), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone. |\n    84\t| `format`, `tickFormat` | Readouts and ticks are formatted separately (F6). |\n    85\t| `scale` (value) | `'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1). Declared, never inferred (F7). |\n    86\t| `missing` (value) | `'gap'` (default) or `'zero'` (F2). |\n    87\t| `parse` (value) | `'number'`, for a text field holding numbers (AX104, AX106). |\n    88\t| `domain` | Author bounds. Widened to zero on bar and area (F1). |\n    89\t\n    90\t### 1.4 Common options\n    91\t\n    92\tSummarized; the declarations are the reference.\n    93\t\n    94\t| Option | Purpose |\n    95\t|---|---|\n    96\t| `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description` | The chart's visible heading and accessible name (A1). `title` may carry a heading `level` and `visible: false`; `labelledBy` points at a heading the page already has. |\n    97\t| `state` | `'ready'`, `'loading'`, `{ error }`, `{ empty }` (L7). |\n    98\t| `legend`, `tooltip`, `table` | On by default; each can be turned off. |\n    99\t| `zoom`, `reference` | Cartesian charts only. |\n   100\t| `compact` | Line, area and bar: marks only, as a static image with a text alternative (spec 3.7). |\n   101\t| `colors` | A fixed color per series name (DX6). |\n   102\t| `messages`, `locale` | Built-in text and formatting locale (A10, F6). |\n   103\t| `strict`, `onDiagnostic` | Diagnostics delivery (section 3.5 of the spec). |\n   104\t\n   105\tThere is no `width`, `height`, `responsive`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colors from CSS, motion from the spec (I5). An unknown key raises AX150 with the closest known name.\n   106\t\n   107\t### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\n   108\t\n   109\t```ts\n   110\tchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                 // options\n   111\tchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', channel: 'Direct', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 });   // rows\n   112\tchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);            // view\n   113\tconst off = chart.on('hover', point => { … });\n   114\tchart.diagnostics;                                   // current, as soon as any call returns\n   115\tchart.destroy();\n   116\t```\n   117\t\n   118\tFinding D3: the ten libraries have seven update idioms. Axylo has three methods, one per kind of change, with one contract.\n   119\t\n   120\t| Rule | Detail |\n   121\t|---|---|\n   122\t| Order | Calls are applied in the order made. `update({ data })` followed by `append` appends to the new data. |\n   123\t| Two phases | Each call validates and normalizes synchronously: diagnostics are current when it returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call. Drawing is deferred to the next animation frame, and calls made before that frame are drawn once. |\n   124\t| Atomic switch | Marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer move to a new data revision together. No frame mixes two revisions. |\n   125\t| Patches | A key present in an `update` replaces the old value whole; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay. No deep merging. |\n   126\t| Rejected changes | A call that throws under `strict` leaves the chart on its last valid state. |\n   127\t| Streaming | `keep` is the number of distinct x values retained. Rows before the last x are accepted through the slower full update and raise AX151. While zoomed to the latest edge the view follows new data; zoomed elsewhere, it stays put. Paused charts buffer (I6). Keyboard focus stays on its datum until that datum leaves the window (A7). |\n   128\t| Teardown | `destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects observers and listeners, and makes later calls no-ops. |\n   129\t\n   130\t### 1.6 Diagnostics\n   131\t\n   132\tThe registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\n   133\t\n   134\t```\n   135\t[axylo AX103] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" has 1 value that is not a finite number (NaN at row 3). Shown as a gap.\n   136\t  Check the calculation that produced it, or send null for \"no data\".\n   137\t[axylo AX106] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" mixes numbers with 1 string (\"40\" at row 4). The string is not plotted.\n   138\t  Convert it to a number where the data is produced.\n   139\t[axylo AX102] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" is absent in 1 row (row 5). Shown as a gap.\n   140\t  Send null if the value is known to be missing.\n   141\t```\n   142\t\n   143\tThe reader of that chart sees \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" (F12).\n   144\t\n   145\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same normalization without a DOM and returns the diagnostics, so a server, a test or a coding agent can check a chart before rendering it.\n   146\t\n   147\tDevelopment and production flavours are selected through package `exports` conditions. The script-tag distribution ships `axylo.js` (development) and `axylo.min.js` (production). Errors and warnings print once per code per page in both; hints only in development.\n   148\t\n   149\t### 1.7 React adapter\n   150\t\n   151\t```tsx\n   152\timport { Line } from '@axylo/react';\n   153\t\n   154\t<Line data={rows} x=\"day\" y=\"sessions\" series=\"channel\" title=\"Daily sessions by channel\" />\n   155\t```\n   156\t\n   157\t| Behavior | Detail |\n   158\t|---|---|\n   159\t| Lifecycle | Create on mount, `destroy` on unmount; correct under StrictMode's setup, cleanup, setup. The file is marked `'use client'`. |\n   160\t| Props to options | Field specs are compared structurally, so an inline `x={{ field: 'ts' }}` does not renormalize on every render. `data` is compared by identity; in development, the same content arriving three times with a new identity raises a hint to memoize it. |\n   161\t| Removed props | A prop that disappears is sent as `undefined`, which restores the default. |\n   162\t| Handlers | Replaced without recreating the chart. |\n   163\t| Streaming | A ref exposes the handle, so `append`, `zoom` and `diagnostics` are reachable. When `data` is controlled, a new `data` prop replaces whatever was appended. |\n   164\t\n   165\tThe adapter is prototyped in M3, before the API freezes, because it is the cheapest way to find out whether the core contract survives a second consumer. It ships in 1.0 because finding 1.1 of the landscape shows where everyday charts are written; that is evidence of where charts are written, not of demand for Axylo.\n   166\t\n   167\t## 2. Architecture\n   168\t\n   169\t### 2.1 Pipeline\n   170\t\n   171\t```\n   172\toptions ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\n   173\t            │ validate   (authoritative) │   scales, visible range         text renderer (DOM)\n   174\t            ▼ diagnostics                ├─► lookup: exact values by x, by point, by ordinal\n   175\t                                         ├─► summary and table\n   176\t                                         └─► focus model\n   177\t```\n   178\t\n   179\tThe **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\n   180\t\n   181\t| Stage | Output | Notes |\n   182\t|---|---|---|\n   183\t| normalize | Frame, diagnostics | The only place raw input is read. Never coerces silently. |\n   184\t| view model | Scales, visible index ranges, series visibility | Domain rules implement F1 and F7. |\n   185\t| lookup | Nearest-x by binary search; nearest-point by a grid index (scatter) | Works on the frame within the visible range; cost bounded independently of the drawing. |\n   186\t| layout | Plot rectangle, tick sets, label boxes, legend and control placement | Implements L1, L3, L8. Takes a text-measurement interface as input. |\n   187\t| scene | Flat list of primitives (`path`, `rect`, `arc`, `rule`, `text`) | Plain data; testable in Node with injected text metrics. |\n   188\t| renderers | Marks; DOM text; accessibility DOM | See 2.3. |\n     1\t<!-- Author: Claude Opus 5.5 (requested effort high), Agent-tool subagent a5529f23e59e816c4. Saved by the coordinator from the hand-back. -->\n     2\t\n     3\t## @axylo/react: what was built and how it was checked\n     4\t\n     5\t`@axylo/react` is built. It has five components, `Line`, `Area`, `Bar`, `Scatter` and `Donut`, each generic in the row type. All my tests pass, and `npx tsc -p packages/react` is clean. The root `npm run typecheck` fails, but only in `packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts`, which another agent is editing (details below). Nothing was committed.\n     6\t\n     7\t**Line to add to the root `typecheck` script:**\n     8\t```\n     9\t\"typecheck\": \"tsc -p packages/axylo --noEmit && tsc -p docs/api && tsc -p packages/react\"\n    10\t```\n    11\t\n    12\t**Measured size** (esbuild minify, `react`, `react-dom`, `react/jsx-runtime` and `axylo` external, gzip level 9):\n    13\t- Production (`__DEV__` false): 1931 B minified, **984 B gzipped**, against the 2048 B limit.\n    14\t- Development: 1228 B gzipped.\n    15\t\n    16\t### Files (all under `/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/`)\n    17\t- **`src/index.ts`**: starts with `'use client'`. Each component uses `forwardRef` (works on React 18 and 19), renders one `<div>`, creates the chart in an effect and destroys it in the cleanup. A second effect computes the patch on every render and calls `chart.update(patch)` once, or not at all.\n    18\t  - Event props and `onDiagnostic` go through a \"latest props\" ref with a stable forwarder, so a new handler never recreates the chart.\n    19\t  - A strict failure is caught in the effect, kept in state and rethrown during render, so an error boundary sees it.\n    20\t  - The `ref` exposes `{ chart }` through a getter: null before mount and after unmount.\n    21\t  - In development it warns once when the same data arrives as a new object three renders in a row.\n    22\t- **`src/patch.ts`**: the pure functions `optionsOf`, `diff`, `equal` and `looksSame`.\n    23\t- **`test/patch.test.ts`**: present, changed, removed, structurally equal, data by identity and handler-only changes, plus a fast-check property.\n    24\t- **`test/server.test.ts`**: renders each component with `react-dom/server` in Node with no DOM. Each gives `<div class=\"axylo\"></div>` and no chart function is called (checked with a spy).\n    25\t- **`test/size.test.ts`**: the gzip size check above.\n    26\t- **`test/types.check.tsx`**: the four required `@ts-expect-error` cases, plus `onZoom` on `Donut`, a misspelling on `Bar` and a category x on `Scatter`. The same mistakes are also written as core calls, to show they fail in the same places. With the markers removed, each line fails for the intended reason.\n    27\t- **`test/browser/`**:\n    28\t  - `react.browser.test.ts`: the 16 browser tests.\n    29\t  - `app.tsx`: the test app, driven through `window.app`.\n    30\t  - `spy-axylo.ts`: wraps the core from source and counts creates, `update` calls and destroys.\n    31\t  - `probe.ts`: counts ResizeObserver callbacks, observers still observing, listeners still registered, and pending animation frames.\n    32\t  - `tree.ts`: the hydration tree, shared by Node and the browser.\n    33\t  - `open.ts`: my wrapper. It pre-bundles the app with `process.env.NODE_ENV`, JSX and the `axylo` → spy redirect, then hands the result to the harness's `openEntry`. The harness itself is unchanged.\n    34\t- **`tsconfig.json`**: now also includes `../axylo/src/core/globals.d.ts`, which declares `__DEV__` for the core source pulled in through `paths`.\n    35\t- **`package.json`**: removed `react-dom` from `peerDependencies`, since the adapter only imports `react` and `axylo`.\n    36\t\n    37\t### What I ran and saw\n    38\t- `npx tsc -p packages/react`: no output (clean).\n    39\t- `npx vitest run packages/react`: `Test Files 3 passed (3)`, `Tests 25 passed (25)`.\n    40\t- `npm test` (full run): `Test Files 41 passed (41)`, `Tests 566 passed (566)`.\n    41\t- `AXYLO_BROWSER=1 npx vitest run packages/react`: `Test Files 1 passed (1)`, `Tests 16 passed (16)`, `Duration 9.78s`. The 16 tests cover:\n    42\t  - mount, update and unmount for each of the five components: the figure appears with its accessible name;\n    43\t  - StrictMode: two creates and one destroy leave one figure; after unmount the container is empty and resizing fires no ResizeObserver callback, with no leaked observers, listeners or pending frames;\n    44\t  - ten re-renders with inline field objects make zero `update` calls, and a real change sends exactly `['y']`;\n    45\t  - a removed `title` sends `{title: undefined}` and gives the same name as a chart that never had a title;\n    46\t  - a removed `tooltip={false}` brings the tooltip back;\n    47\t  - the data warning fires once;\n    48\t  - the latest `onHover` is called, with the same figure element and no update;\n    49\t  - `append` takes the line from 10 to 11 points, and a new `data` prop brings it back to the original 10;\n    50\t  - a strict chart rejected at creation and at update is caught by the boundary and recovers;\n    51\t  - two charts in one tree stay independent;\n    52\t  - hydration produces no console errors and no recoverable errors, plus a control showing that altered markup is reported.\n    53\t- `npm run typecheck`: fails at `packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts(862,99): error TS2322: Type 'string | boolean' is not assignable to type 'boolean'`. That file is not mine.\n    54\t- **Mutation check**: I broke the adapter seven ways, one at a time, and restored it each time (confirmed with diff; a clean re-run followed). Six of the seven were caught by the browser tests:\n    55\t  - identity comparison instead of structural;\n    56\t  - no destroy in cleanup;\n    57\t  - handlers bound at creation;\n    58\t  - no rethrow;\n    59\t  - removed keys dropped;\n    60\t  - the data warning firing too early.\n    61\t  The seventh, a forced hydration mismatch, was caught only by the Node-side markup assertion in the hydration test. The browser-side hydration check is backed by the control test instead.\n    62\t\n    63\t### Not done or not verified\n    64\t- **React 18 is not tested**: only React 19.3.1 is installed. Support is expected from the design (`forwardRef`, no React 19-only APIs), not observed.\n    65\t- **No `dist` build exists for the adapter**, because `scripts/build.mjs` is not mine. A real build must define `__DEV__`, as the core's build does, and must keep the `'use client'` banner, since esbuild drops directives when bundling.\n    66\t- `packages/react/test/browser/.out/axylo-react-app.js` (about 1.7 MB, generated by the tests) is not covered by `.gitignore`. Suggested line: `packages/react/test/browser/.out/`.\n    67\t- No contract change is needed.\n    68\t\n    69\t### Assumptions where the spec was ambiguous\n    70\t- Every option except `data` is compared structurally, not only the listed ones. Functions inside options, such as `format: v => …`, are compared by identity, so an inline formatter does cause an update.\n    71\t- `{a: undefined}` and `{}` count as different inside an object. At the top level, an absent prop and one explicitly set to `undefined` count as the same.\n    72\t- `onDiagnostic` is sent only when it appears or disappears; replacing one function with another sends nothing.\n    73\t- `Donut` has no `onZoom` prop, because `DonutOptions` has no `zoom`.\n    74\t- The container always carries the class `axylo`, even on the server, so that React does not strip it when `className` changes.\n    75\t- Recovering after an error means the boundary remounts the component: a throw during render discards the failed instance. The test boundary resets when its `data` prop changes.\n    76\t- The data warning is a `console.warn`, printed once per page.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
