te and number x axes. 'touch' also captures pinch and two-finger drag on touch screens. */\n   112\t  zoom?: boolean | 'touch';\n   113\t  reference?: readonly Reference[];\n   114\t}\n   115\t\n   116\t/** Line, area and bar only. Marks only, as a static image with a text alternative: no axes, legend, toolbar, table, zoom or tooltip. */\n   117\texport interface CompactOption { compact?: boolean }\n   118\t\n   119\texport type LineOptions<Row> = CommonOptions<Row> & CartesianOptions & CompactOption & Encoding<Row>;\n   120\texport type AreaOptions<Row> = CommonOptions<Row> & CartesianOptions & CompactOption & Encoding<Row> & { stacked?: boolean };\n   121\texport type BarOptions<Row> = CommonOptions<Row> & CartesianOptions & CompactOption & Encoding<Row> & { layout?: 'grouped' | 'stacked'; horizontal?: boolean };\n   122\t/** Scatter relates two measures: its x is a number, an instant or a date, never a category. */\n   123\texport type ScatterXField<Row> = XKey<Row> | ({ field: XKey<Row>; type?: 'number' | 'time' | 'date'; ordered?: never } & Omit<XFieldOptions, 'type' | 'ordered'>);\n   124\texport type ScatterOptions<Row> = CommonOptions<Row> & CartesianOptions & { x: ScatterXField<Row>; y: ValueField<Row>; series?: SeriesKey<Row> };\n   125\texport type DonutOptions<Row> = CommonOptions<Row> & { label: SeriesKey<Row>; value: ValueField<Row> };\n   126\t\n   127\t// ---------- handle ----------\n   128\t\n   129\texport interface Point<Row> { series: string; x: XValue; y: number | null; row: Row | null; index: number }\n   130\t\n   131\texport interface ChartEvents<Row> {\n   132\t  hover: Point<Row> | null;\n   133\t  select: Point<Row>;\n   134\t  zoom: readonly [XValue, XValue] | null;\n   135\t}\n   136\t\n   137\texport interface Chart<Row, Options> {\n   138\t  /**\n   139\t   * Changes options. Keys present replace the old value; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay.\n   140\t   * Validation and diagnostics are synchronous (a `strict` chart throws here and keeps its last valid state); drawing happens on the next frame.\n   141\t   */\n   142\t  update(patch: Partial<Options>): void;\n   143\t  /** Adds rows at the end. `keep` = number of distinct x values retained; older ones are dropped. */\n   144\t  append(rows: readonly Row[], options?: { keep?: number }): void;\n   145\t  /** Sets the visible x range; `null` shows everything. */\n   146\t  zoom(domain: readonly [XValue, XValue] | null): void;\n   147\t  on<E extends keyof ChartEvents<Row>>(event: E, listener: (payload: ChartEvents<Row>[E]) => void): () => void;\n   148\t  /** Diagnostics for the current options and data. Up to date as soon as create, update or append returns. */\n   149\t  readonly diagnostics: readonly Diagnostic[];\n   150\t  destroy(): void;\n   151\t}\n   152\t\n   153\texport type Target = string | HTMLElement;\n   140\t| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13 |\n   141\t| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n   142\t| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\n   143\t| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\n   144\t| A8 | axe-core reports no violations on any fixture in any state, including `heading-order` and `scrollable-region-focusable`. | CI | R11 |\n   145\t| A9 | A conformance table lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 AA success criterion, the test that covers it, and what the author must supply (a meaningful title or `labelledBy`, series names, units, the page's own structure). It also states the limits in section 3.8. | Published with each release; a CI check that every listed test exists | R11 |\n   146\t| A10 | Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"` when the document language is not English. | Test on a `lang=\"de\"` page | WCAG 3.1.2 |\n   147\t\n   148\t### 3.5 Diagnostic registry\n   149\t\n   150\tLevel decides delivery. **Error**: the chart or a series cannot be drawn; the chart shows its error state. **Warning**: something is not drawn as given; the reader sees the note in F12. **Hint**: advice for the author. Errors and warnings print once per code per page in every build; hints print in development builds only. `strict: true` throws on errors and warnings found while validating a create, `update` or `append` call, synchronously from that call. A diagnostic that can only arise later (a contrast check after the page's CSS changes, a custom formatter that throws when a table page is built, a label that stops fitting after a resize) is reported when it is found and is never thrown. Every diagnostic names the chart; those about data name the field and the first row indexes in the caller's own data.\n   151\t\n   152\t| Code | Level | Condition | What is drawn |\n   153\t|---|---|---|---|\n   154\t| AX100 | Error | Typed columns have different lengths | Error state; the message names the columns and their lengths |\n   155\t| AX101 | Error | A named field is not a key of any row | Error state; the message lists the keys that exist and the closest match |\n   156\t| AX102 | Warning | A field is absent (`undefined`) in some rows | Gaps |\n   157\t| AX103 | Warning | A value is `NaN` or infinite | Gap |\n   158\t| AX104 | Hint | Every non-missing value of a value field is a plain decimal string (`'40'`, `'-1.5'`) | Parsed and drawn; the message suggests `parse: 'number'`. An empty string counts as missing |\n   159\t| AX105 | Hint | Steps are absent on a regular time, date, wall-clock or number axis | Gaps. When a series would need more absent steps than twice its observations (and more than 1,000), it is treated as irregular instead: no gaps are created and the hint says so |\n   160\t| AX106 | Warning | A value field mixes numbers with strings, or holds strings that are not plain decimals (`'1,234'`, `'n/a'`) | The strings are not drawn |\n   161\t| AX107 | Warning | A date or time cannot be parsed | That row is not drawn |\n   162\t| AX108 | Error | Duplicate x within a series, or duplicate donut labels | That series, or the donut, is not drawn |\n   163\t| AX109 | Hint | Numbers on an axis declared `time` look like seconds, or a numeric x looks like epoch milliseconds without `type: 'time'` | As declared |\n   164\t| AX110 | Warning | An author domain on bar or area excluded zero | Domain widened to zero |\n   165\t| AX111 | Hint | Instants look like calendar dates | As instants, zone shown |\n   166\t| AX112 | Hint | Date-time strings have no offset | As written |\n   167\t| AX120 | Error | Negative donut value | Error state |\n   168\t| AX121 | Warning | More than eight donut slices | Seven largest plus \"Other (n)\" |\n   169\t| AX130 | Warning | More than eight series | All series, with dash or marker differences |\n   170\t| AX140 | Warning | No `title` and no `labelledBy` | Generated name |\n   171\t| AX141 | Warning | Palette below 3:1 on the resolved background | As themed |\n   172\t| AX142 | Hint | Plot too small to draw | Summary and table control |\n   173\t| AX143 | Hint | Labels hidden after every remedy | Note shown to the reader |\n   218\t## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n   219\t\n   220\t| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n   221\t|---|---|---|---|\n   222\t| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm, by script tag and in React. No required option besides data and fields. | Three CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\n   223\t| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\n   224\t| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\n   225\t| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\n   226\t| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\n   227\t| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n   228\t| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets, provisional until M1 measures a working slice: 40 KiB gzipped for everything, 25 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, 2 KiB for the React adapter, CSS included. | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n   229\t| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Works under React StrictMode, including setup, cleanup and setup again. Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n   230\t| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n   231\t| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n   232\t| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n   233\t| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n   234\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
