{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"agent_message","text":"**B for shipping `be123ee`.** The documented `import { Line } from '@axylo/react'` fails. The most consequential runtime defect is the gap between React’s layout and passive effects. A parent can receive a ref whose chart is still `null`, or append to a chart whose new controlled data has committed in React but has yet to reach the core.\n\n### Findings, most severe first\n\n1. **B · The package exports files that the build never produces. Ran and inspected.** Node returned `ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND` for `@axylo/react/dist/index.js`. The declaration entry is missing too. The root build targets only `packages/axylo`, and the adapter’s TypeScript project has `noEmit`. The size test nevertheless passes at **984 B gzip** because it bundles `src/index.ts` in memory. With the installed esbuild, that direct entry retained `\"use client\"`; a bundle that re-exported `Line` from another entry lost it. Build the adapter JS and declarations, select the development hint appropriately, then import, type-check, server-render and measure the **packed export**. Assert the directive on the shipped entry. This blocks DX1, DX7, DX8 and DX10 release verification. [Package exports](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/package.json:8) · [Build](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/scripts/build.mjs:5) · [Size test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/size.test.ts:10)\n\n2. **M · The ref becomes observable before creation and before updates. Reasoned from the adapter and installed React 19.3.1.** `useImperativeHandle` publishes during layout. Creation and patching happen in passive effects. On mount, a callback ref or parent layout effect sees `handle.chart === null` and receives no notification when it becomes usable. On update, commit `data=B`, then call `append(row)` in the parent layout effect. The core receives `append` against A, followed by `update({data:B})`, which erases the row. A diagnostics read in that layout effect likewise describes A. The core obeys §1.5 call order throughout. Synchronize creation and accepted props in the child’s client layout phase before publishing the usable handle. Test this sequence without waiting for frames, including on Suspense reveal. This affects §1.7 streaming and DX5. [Adapter](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:53) · [React’s imperative-handle implementation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/node_modules/react-dom/cjs/react-dom-client.development.js:9111)\n\n3. **M · A committed handler change can still call the previous handler. Reasoned.** Commit `onDiagnostic=B` over A, then trigger a synchronous diagnostic through the ref from a parent layout effect. The stable forwarder still reads A. Removing `onHover` has the same window in which the removed callback can fire. Once passive effects run, present → absent → present works, and diagnostics from the adapter’s own `update` see the new handler. Publish the latest handlers in the child’s client layout phase before externally observable chart calls. Add replacement, removal and reinstatement tests that fire during the commit. This affects §1.7 handlers and §1.5 synchronous diagnostic delivery. [Forwarder and ref update](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:51)\n\n4. **M · Explicit `undefined` can fail to restore a default. Ran the diff and modeled the public ref sequence.** Mount with `title` absent, call `chart.update({title:'Live'})` through the ref, then render `title={undefined}`. `diff({}, {title:undefined})` returns `null`, so `Live` survives. The reverse own-key transition also returns `null`. The test explicitly requires both results. Compare own-key presence as well as values and send an own `title: undefined` on the transition. Test against a prior imperative update so the reset has an observable effect. This violates §1.5 patch semantics and §1.7 removal semantics. [Diff](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:28) · [Test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/patch.test.ts:45)\n\n5. **M · Structural comparison can overflow before core validation. Ran.** Two separately allocated field objects with the same `field` and a self-referential metadata property produced `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`. A structurally typed field variable can carry that extra property. Two mistakenly supplied React elements with cyclic props also overflowed; two ordinary ownerless elements compared equal. `diff` runs outside the `update` try block, so the adapter can take down the chart with its own exception. Bound comparison to supported option shapes or track visited pairs and treat unsupported objects, including React elements, as opaque. Add a regression that reaches the update path. This affects §1.7 field comparison and DX4 diagnostic delivery. [Comparator](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:39) · [Call site](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:87)\n\n6. **M · Filling a hole in an option array can silently discard the change. Ran.** `diff({y:Array(1)}, {y:['v']})` returned `null`. Reversing the arguments emitted a patch. `a.every(...)` skips the previous hole, so correcting a sparse wide `y` or `reference` list can leave the chart on its old state. Compare indexed presence and values symmetrically, and test a sparse-to-dense correction in both directions. This violates whole-value replacement in §1.5. [Array comparison](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:43)\n\n7. **M · Container attributes go to chart validation and disappear from the DOM. Ran SSR, `optionsOf` and TypeScript probes.** `id`, `aria-label` and `data-testid` were absent from the server div and present in core options. TypeScript rejected `id` and `onClick` but accepted the hyphenated JSX attributes. Under the specified AX150 behavior, an ordinary `data-*` attribute can therefore reject a strict chart. Omitting `id` also prevents the documented element-id fallback for diagnostic names. This produces the same omission on server and client; it does not require a hydration mismatch to fail. Define the supported host attributes, route them to the div on both sides, and preserve validation of genuine unknown chart options. §1.7 does not promise every div prop, but forwarding these attributes to chart validation has observable DX3/DX4 consequences. [Prop types](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:19) · [Div](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:100) · [Option routing](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:9)\n\n8. **M · Importing only `Line` retains all five chart factories. Ran an in-memory consumer bundle.** The output constructed all five components and imported all five core factories. With distinct stand-in implementations, all five survived bundling. Marking the component factory calls pure in memory reduced the retained implementations to `line`. `sideEffects: false` alone does not eliminate those calls inside a used module. Preserve pure annotations through the emitted distribution or split the component entries, and check a packed `Line`-only consumer. This affects the plan’s per-chart tree-shaking goal and DX7. [Component exports](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:106)\n\n9. **m · An equal replacement can incur a full traversal on every subsequent render. Ran.** Replace a large `colors` record or `reference` list with an equal new object, then keep that new object stable. The null-patch return leaves `state.applied` pointing to the older object. In Node, 20 comparisons of 20,000 entries took about 72 ms for `colors` and 25 ms for `reference`. That is comparator time alone; the cost of a real core update was unmeasured. Record the latest successfully compared props on a no-op, and reserve unbounded structural work for options that require it. Same-reference in-place mutations are also invisible, so the comparison assumes immutable option values. `colors` is a record in the public API. [No-op return](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:87) · [Traversal](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:45)\n\n10. **m · Object enumeration can lose a field change or produce the wrong reset value. Ran.** Two plain field specs with different non-enumerable `field` values compared equal. Removing an own `toString` option produced the inherited function as the patch value. `optionsOf` also copied inherited enumerable props. These paths bypass the intended §1.5 replacement/reset or DX3 unknown-option handling. Compare the documented properties of field specs, enumerate own props when routing, and use own-key checks or prototype-free dictionaries for patch lookup. [Option copying](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:15) · [Lookup](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:30)\n\n11. **m · The memoization hint can advise against a real data update. Ran.** Equal-length arrays with identical endpoints and a changing middle row returned `true` from `looksSame`. Equal dates reconstructed in endpoint rows and freshly allocated typed columns with identical values returned `false`. After three qualifying replacements, the warning asserts that the data was the same. The module-wide `hinted` flag then suppresses hints for other charts. Confirm content before issuing that claim, or word the hint as a sampling heuristic and specify its scope. The §1.7 hint is affected. Actual `data` patches still use identity. [Sampling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/patch.ts:53) · [Warning](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:79)\n\n12. **m · Deferring an effect error to component state can lose it. Reasoned from React’s commit-error path.** The comment that an effect throw never reaches a boundary is incorrect for a synchronous effect setup. If the chart effect catches a strict error and a parent removes the child in the same passive-effect batch, the child can disappear before it renders `failure`; a surviving boundary then receives nothing from this path. Let synchronous setup/update exceptions propagate to React’s commit-error handling, and test original-object identity with a boundary that stays mounted. In ordinary recovery, the current code does throw the original object, the fallback unmounts the failed instance, and reset mounts a fresh one. Changing props alone cannot clear `failure` on that instance. [Error handling](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:49) · [React’s boundary capture](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/node_modules/react-dom/cjs/react-dom-client.development.js:20217)\n\n13. **m · A setup failure after successful creation has no cleanup path. Reasoned; conditional on core behavior.** If listener registration throws, including through an immediately invoked user listener, the effect exits before returning its disposer. The successfully created chart then has no owner to destroy it. §1.5 promises teardown when `destroy()` is called; it does not specify registration-time event delivery. Destroy the local chart before propagating a setup failure. Separately, a factory that allocates resources and then throws must roll them back itself because the adapter receives no handle. Neither case requires `on` or `destroy` to throw a *strict validation* error. [Creation and listener installation](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:61)\n\n14. **m · The teardown test observes an already settled chart. Reasoned from the harness.** `render()` waits for frames and `ready()` before unmount. A mutation that lets a pending draw run after teardown can pass because the draw has already happened. The destroy spy stores a boolean, so double destruction also passes. The listener probe excludes detached nodes and all `once` listeners, and the spy retains chart elements. Count destroys, unmount before the pending frame, and assert against the saved handle and scheduled callback. The current test establishes less than the DX5 teardown and leak criteria. [Waits](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:55) · [Spy](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/spy-axylo.ts:26) · [Probe](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/probe.ts:69)\n\n### Test coverage and mutations the seven reported checks missed\n\n| Tests examined | What their assertions distinguish, and what can still pass |\n|---|---|\n| `optionsOf`, `diff`, `equal`, `looksSame` | The examples distinguish ordinary additions, changes, removal of defined values, whole nested replacements, function changes and handler filtering. Deep-equality assertions can accept a copied `data` value. The property uses production `equal` as its oracle and compares indexed values, so it can share the comparator’s defect and miss own-key differences. Add independent presence, identity and symmetry assertions. [Patch tests](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/patch.test.ts:13) |\n| Five browser lifecycle cases, StrictMode, independence | The cases check factory kind, title updates, destruction and isolation between two charts. A render-phase write of `latest.current`, a duplicate destroy, or a stale saved handle can pass. Exercise an abandoned transition, an actual key change, and a saved getter across cleanup. [Lifecycle cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:82) |\n| Inline options, removed title, removed tooltip, memoization hint | The inline test counts updates. Title checks the own reset key and generated name. Tooltip checks restored behavior. None exercises removal of required fields. The hint test would accept a warning one or two replacements late because it checks only after three more renders. Assert on each replacement and include changing middle rows. [Prop cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:135) |\n| Handlers, ref, strict errors | Hover is exercised after settling. A broken `select` or `zoom` forwarder, stale layout-phase callback, or broken diagnostic removal can pass. The ref test always appends to a ready chart. The boundary test checks message text and remount counts; wrapping the error or advancing `applied` before a rejected update can pass because recovery creates a new instance. [Handler, ref and error cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:213) |\n| Server, hydration and size | The five SSR cases establish empty markup and zero factory calls. The class/style case checks exact HTML. The directive assertion reads source. The hydration control detects deliberately altered markup, but successful hydration never checks reuse of the original div and both sides use source. All these tests can pass with a broken package entry. Save the server node for an identity assertion and run the distribution checks through the package name. [Server cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/server.test.ts:23) · [Hydration](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/react.browser.test.ts:312) · [Size](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/size.test.ts:12) |\n| Type fixtures | I removed all 11 suppressions **in memory**. Every marked line failed. The misspellings, text value, donut options and scatter category produced the intended diagnostics. The two wide-`y` cases first complain about widened `string[]`; using `as const` isolates the forbidden combination. Every directive can mask another error on its line. Pair each negative case with the same otherwise-valid expression. JSX inferred rows and typed columns. `createElement(Line, …)` and `memo(Line)` lost row inference; specializing `Line<Row>` worked. [Type fixtures](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/types.check.tsx:32) |\n\n### Verification and contract limits\n\n`npm run typecheck` passed in the current checkout, including `test/types.check.tsx`. I ran the adapter’s Node suite with filesystem writes disabled, native config loading, threads, and both Vitest caches disabled. Its final lines were:\n\n```text\nTest Files  3 passed (3)\n     Tests  25 passed (25)\n  Start at  04:25:38\n  Duration  518ms (transform 65%, import 24%, tests 10%, worker 2%)\n```\n\nI changed no files. Browser behavior, Fast Refresh and React 18 were assessed by reading. Only React 19.3.1 ran. I did not inspect the changing core implementation. The adapter assumes the target’s `axylo` class is sufficient when React rewrites `className`. The browser spy also calls extracted `update` and `destroy` methods without their receiver; §1.5 allows a conforming implementation to use `this`. Preserve the receiver in the spy. The manifest has no direct `react-dom` peer, which matches the adapter’s imports; the workspace lockfile still lists that peer and should be synchronized. A consumer still needs a DOM renderer to mount or server-render the components. [Host class](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/src/index.ts:98) · [Spy](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/react/test/browser/spy-axylo.ts:26) · [Lockfile](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/package-lock.json:2271)\n\nNo core contract change is needed for these fixes. The fixed public `Chart` exposes `zoom()` and `on('zoom', …)` for **every** chart. I verified that a donut ref accepts both, while donut JSX rejects `zoom` and `onZoom`, and `donutRef.chart.update({zoom:true})` is rejected. A chart-specific handle that excludes those methods would require a deliberate public API decision. [Public handle](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/public.ts:137)\n\n### Areas examined and found sound\n\n- **Committed renders.** Reasoned. Creation, diffing and writes to `applied` occur in effects. An abandoned render publishes no options, callback change or patch.\n- **Normal lifecycle.** Reasoned. StrictMode deliberately creates twice with cleanup between setups. A key change disposes the old instance. A Fast Refresh remount or effect replay pairs successful setup with cleanup. The adapter’s normal path clears `live` before destruction. A caller who explicitly destroys the raw chart can still cause later adapter updates to reach it; §1.5 makes those calls no-ops.\n- **Suspense and ref identity.** Reasoned for installed React 19. A Suspense fallback disconnects the layout ref while the existing chart’s passive effect can remain connected. Reveal can publish a new wrapper around that chart. With an unchanged ref, ordinary rerenders keep the wrapper stable. A saved getter reads `null` after actual passive cleanup; during StrictMode replay it can subsequently see the replacement chart.\n- **Strict rejection and recovery.** Reasoned. A thrown `update` leaves `applied` at the last successful options, consistent with §1.5 rollback. The latest handler ref can already reflect the rejected render. Boundary remount gets fresh state. A strict `append` through the raw ref throws synchronously to its caller, and an event-handler caller must catch it there.\n- **Ordinary patches.** Ran. Removing previously defined `data`, `x`, `y` and `onDiagnostic` emitted own keys with `undefined`. Missing required fields then reach core validation. Event props use subscriptions, with absent handlers becoming no-ops after synchronization. The diagnostic forwarder is installed for a supplied callback and cleared on removal.\n- **Ordinary comparison.** Ran. Dense nested values replace whole options. Dates compare by timestamp, including two invalid dates. `NaN` compares equal and signed zeros differ. Functions, maps, typed arrays and class instances compare by identity. Data replacement uses identity; only the development hint inspects row endpoints or column references.\n- **Source SSR and option types.** Ran. All five components rendered an empty div with deterministic class/style, a null server ref and zero factory calls. Each JSX prop type uses its corresponding public options type, including area stacking, bar layout, scatter field restrictions and donut fields. By inspection, the hooks and `forwardRef` used here are available in React 18."}}
